// Answers the model gave on real pull requests, which the tests of isClean read.

// Analysis of the checks it ran, then the verdict (a protobase review).
export const analysisThenClean = "I worked through every changed library file (packages/) and ran the four generic-framework checks on each, then traced the security-critical paths (tenant scoping, org-role provenance, the definitions FK, the migration, and the config refusal). No app-specific names, no schema guessing, no single-app hard-codes: `organization_id` is the framework's own declared tenant column for this feature, and everything else reads declared config (`userTables`, `auth.organizations`), relations, and field types. The org role is read from the member row per request (`organizationRoleOf(member)`), never from the token claim, so a demotion applies on the next request as claimed.\n\nI verified the security model end to end: a `.tenant` resource over `organization_id` plus `organizationAdminsOf(definition.organization_id)` (which requires the session's org to equal the definition's *and* owner/admin/superuser) means a user in org A gets 404 and no `/meta`/OpenAPI trace of org B's table; the `(table_id, tenant_key) → tables(id, tenant_key)` FK and the `lockedTable` `tenant_key` match stop a field from reaching another org's table; the `table_name` generated column plus the 42-byte `maxOrganizationNameBytes` keep `t<id>_<name>` within 63 bytes; and the pre-organizations migration (drop `tables_name_key`, add generated columns, partial unique indexes, fields FK) is consistent and tested against a real old schema. The `checkTokenStanding`/`checkUserStanding` return-shape change is consumed correctly by its only production callers (`ownStanding`, `grantStanding`), and the config refusal (`authenticate.organizations` without `auth.organizations`) is sound.\n\nNo issues found."

// The verdict, then notes it says rise to no finding (a protobase review).
export const cleanThenNotes = "No issues found.\n\nI worked through every changed library file under `packages/` with the four generic-framework checks and the project rules. The changes are confined to the user-tables feature's own schemas, access rules, and scaffolding — no app-domain names, no schema guessing from field names, no hard-coded magic values outside the feature's own `table_definitions` / `user_tables` schemas. The `organization_id` column name is a fixed part of the user-tables feature's own DDL, not an inference about an app's schema.\n\nThe access model is sound: `organizationAdminsOf` compares the session's organization id against the definition's, and `organizationAdmins` requires an `owner`/`admin` org role or the `admin` app role (superuser). The role is read from the member row on every request via `checkTokenStanding`, never from the token's `org_role` claim, so a demotion applies on the next request. The `.tenant` mechanism on both the served tables and the definitions resources ensures the server sets `organization_id` from the session and filters every query by it; a body that names it is refused (tested, 400).\n\nThe migration path is correct: `organizationsSql` adds the new generated columns and drops the old `tables_name_key` constraint, replacing it with two partial unique indexes; `fieldsOrganizationSql` adds the composite foreign key. The `current()` check in `schema.ts` detects the `table_name` column to decide whether the migration is needed. The `lockedTable` query uses `tenant_key = coalesce(…)` to scope the lock to the field's own organization, matching the foreign key.\n\nThe config check in `checkUserTables` correctly distinguishes the app's own organizations (`auth.organizations`) from trusting an external auth server's tokens (`authenticate.organizations` alone), and the error message names both settings.\n\nThe docs follow the features grammar, the new `## Organizations` section starts with a `:::caution[Beta]` aside, the features entry links to the existing heading, and the removed `<gap>` and added `<gap>` for members are consistent with the stability rules. The two commits are valid Conventional Commits `feat` entries with docs shipped alongside.\n\nOne minor inconsistency I noted but does not rise to a finding: `reservedFieldNames` in `names.ts` reserves `organization_id` for all user-table fields, while `checkDefinition` reserves it only for organization tables. In practice the API validation (`nameProblem`) is the primary path and prevents creating such a field, so the `checkDefinition` leniency is unreachable through the API. A non-organization table that somehow already had an `organization_id` field (created before this PR) would fail at the DDL step when a new field is added, but this requires a user to have named a field `organization_id` in a non-organization table, which the new API validation now blocks."

// A section of generic-framework checks that found none, then a Low finding (a protobase review).
export const noneThenLowFinding = "## Generic-framework findings\n\nNone. I worked through every changed library file against the four checks. The new `packages/server/src/user-tables/definitions-views.ts` and its `fieldTypeLabels` are the framework's own user‑tables feature (a documented framework feature, not a demo domain), and `translated-views.ts` is a generic catalogue‑lookup mechanism keyed off `model.table` — no app schema is guessed, no field name is hard‑coded, and the new behaviour (`notOnCreate`, `creatorsOnly`, `resource: '{field}'`, the two icons) is all declared config/extension points, not app branches. The `groupLabels` entries `table_definitions`/`user_tables` are the framework labelling its own feature schemas, consistent with the existing `core`/`sales` entries, not one app's schema.\n\n## Other findings\n\n**Low — PR title exceeds the 92‑character header limit**\n- Where: the pull request title, `feat: people who may add tables make a table and its fields in the admin app, also on a phone`.\n- What is wrong: the title is 93 characters (20 words totalling 74 letters + 19 spaces). CONTRIBUTING.md's commit‑message rule states \"the header is at most 92 characters\", and the `pr-title` workflow checks the squash‑merge message the PR title produces (title + ` (#<number>)`). At 93, the title alone is over the 92 limit, and with the suffix it is 101, over the 100 the rule exists to keep.\n- Concrete failure: the `pr-title` check on this PR fails, so the squash merge is blocked until the title is shortened.\n- Fix: trim at least one character, e.g. `feat: people who may add tables make a table and its fields in the admin app` (drop `, also on a phone`) or reword to fit 92.\n\nNo other real problems found. The `notOnCreate` server check, the `creatorsOnly` nav gate, the `resource: '{field}'` link (server refusal of filter/order/values, UI gating on `/meta`), and the built‑in‑view translation all behave as documented and are covered by the added tests; the phone stories and `fake-user-tables.ts` are test fixtures, and the unverified `/meta` refetch timing is explicitly called out as a story limitation rather than a framework change."
